# CSS Transitions: The Fundamentals 🔄

A transition smoothly animates the change of a CSS property between two states (e.g. normal state and `:hover` state).

```css_not
.button {
  background-color: #2563eb;
  /* transition: [property] [duration] [timing-function] [delay] */
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease;
}

.button:hover {
  background-color: #1d4ed8;
}
```

Without the `transition` declaration, the background color would snap instantly. With `transition`, it interpolates smoothly over 300 milliseconds!

# Transition Properties Breakdown ⚙️

You can configure each aspect of a transition individually:

- `transition-property`: Which properties to animate (e.g. `transform`, `opacity`, `background-color`).
- `transition-duration`: How long the animation takes (e.g. `0.2s`, `350ms`).
- `transition-timing-function`: Speed curve (e.g. `ease`, `linear`, `ease-in-out`).
- `transition-delay`: Wait time before the animation starts (e.g. `0.1s`).

```css_not
.card {
  transition-property: transform, box-shadow;
  transition-duration: 0.3s, 0.3s;
  transition-timing-function: ease-out;
}
```

# CSS Transforms (`transform`) 📐

Transforms modify the visual coordinate space of an element without disrupting neighboring layouts:

```css_not
.box-translated {
  transform: translate(20px, -10px); /* X and Y offsets */
}

.box-scaled {
  transform: scale(1.1); /* Scales up to 110% of size */
}

.box-rotated {
  transform: rotate(45deg); /* Rotates 45 degrees clockwise */
}

/* Combine multiple transforms! */
.card:hover {
  transform: translateY(-8px) scale(1.02);
}
```

# Multi-Stage Motion with `@keyframes` 🎞️

While transitions only animate from state A to state B, `@keyframes` allows multi-stage, autonomous animation sequences that run on loop!

```css_not
@keyframes pulse {
  0% {
    transform: scale(1);
    opacity: 1;
  }
  50% {
    transform: scale(1.15);
    opacity: 0.7;
  }
  100% {
    transform: scale(1);
    opacity: 1;
  }
}

.pulsing-badge {
  animation: pulse 2s infinite ease-in-out;
}
```

# The `animation` Properties 🎛️

```css_not
/* Syntax: animation: [name] [duration] [timing] [delay] [iteration-count] [direction] [fill-mode] */
.loader {
  animation: spin 1s linear infinite;
}
```

## Key Properties:
- `animation-iteration-count`: Number of times to play (`1`, `3`, `infinite`).
- `animation-direction`: `normal`, `reverse`, `alternate` (plays forward then backward!).
- `animation-play-state`: `running` or `paused` (great for pausing carousels on hover).

# Practical Micro-Interaction: Elevated Card 🃏

```html_not
<article class="hover-card">
  <h3>Interactive Component</h3>
  <p>Hover to see elevation physics.</p>
</article>
```

```css_not
.hover-card {
  background: #ffffff;
  padding: 24px;
  border-radius: 16px;
  box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);
  transition: transform 0.25s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1),
              box-shadow 0.25s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
  will-change: transform, box-shadow;
}

.hover-card:hover {
  transform: translateY(-6px);
  box-shadow: 0 20px 25px -5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1),
              0 10px 10px -5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.04);
}
```

# Practical Component: CSS Loading Spinner 🌀

Create a performant loading spinner with pure CSS:

```html_not
<div class="spinner"></div>
```

```css_not
.spinner {
  width: 40px;
  height: 40px;
  border: 4px solid #e2e8f0;
  border-top-color: #3b82f6; /* Colored accent indicator */
  border-radius: 50%;
  animation: spin 0.8s linear infinite;
}

@keyframes spin {
  to {
    transform: rotate(360deg);
  }
}
```

# Modern UI: Skeleton Loading Shimmer 💀

Skeleton placeholders give users immediate visual perception of fast loading:

```css_not
.skeleton {
  background: linear-gradient(
    90deg,
    #f1f5f9 25%,
    #e2e8f0 50%,
    #f1f5f9 75%
  );
  background-size: 200% 100%;
  animation: shimmer 1.5s infinite;
  border-radius: 4px;
}

@keyframes shimmer {
  0%   { background-position: 200% 0; }
  100% { background-position: -200% 0; }
}
```

# Staggered Animation Delays 🪜

Staggering the appearance of list items creates a premium native app feel:

```css_not
.list-item {
  animation: fadeInUp 0.5s ease-out forwards;
  opacity: 0;
}

.list-item:nth-child(1) { animation-delay: 0.1s; }
.list-item:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: 0.2s; }
.list-item:nth-child(3) { animation-delay: 0.3s; }
.list-item:nth-child(4) { animation-delay: 0.4s; }

@keyframes fadeInUp {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(20px);
  }
  to {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: translateY(0);
  }
}
```

# Accessibility: `prefers-reduced-motion` ♿

Vestibular disorders and motion sensitivities can trigger vertigo or nausea from excessive animations.

Always respect user OS preferences:

```css_not
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
  }
}
```

When users turn on "Reduce Motion" in Windows/macOS/iOS/Android, this snippet disables motion while maintaining instant state functionality!

# Optimizing with `will-change` 🚀

The `will-change` property hints to the browser that an element will animate soon, allowing it to prepare GPU layers ahead of time:

```css_not
.animating-box {
  will-change: transform, opacity;
}
```

> ⚠️ **Warning:** Never put `will-change` on hundreds of elements at once! Creating excessive GPU layers wastes device memory and degrades battery life.

# 3D Transforms: Card Flip 🎴

```html_not
<div class="card-container">
  <div class="flipper">
    <div class="front">Front Side</div>
    <div class="back">Back Side</div>
  </div>
</div>
```

```css_not
.card-container {
  perspective: 1000px; /* Establishes 3D perspective distance */
}

.flipper {
  transition: transform 0.6s;
  transform-style: preserve-3d;
}

.card-container:hover .flipper {
  transform: rotateY(180deg);
}

.front, .back {
  backface-visibility: hidden; /* Hides back when facing away */
}

.back {
  transform: rotateY(180deg);
}
```
